  "summary": "Kazimir Malevich's abstraction of pure geometric forms floating in white space, art reduced to sensation.",
  "description": "Malevich announced Suprematism in 1915 with Black Square, painting \"the supremacy of pure feeling\" through colored squares, circles and bars on white. The movement abandoned depiction entirely, treating geometry as spiritual physics. It fed into Constructivism, influenced László Moholy-Nagy and modern abstraction, and its black square endures as a meme of radical reduction. Soviet policy later condemned it as formalist.",
  "culturalContext": "Suprematism — Kazimir Malevich's 1915 invention, announced with the Black Square at 0.10 exhibition — reduced painting to \"the supremacy of pure feeling\": floating geometric primitives in weightless white space, no horizon, no object, no metaphor. The Black Square (1915), White on White (1918), and the \"vkus\" (taste) polemics made it the most radical zero-point of modern art; it fed UNOVIS (with El Lissitzky's Prouns), influenced constructivist graphics and Bauhaus abstraction, and was suppressed under Stalin as formalism.",
